// **A `Duration` is `Display` but not `Comparable`, and the checker says so.**
//
// A calendar span has no total order without a reference date — `PT1M` against `P1D` depends on
// which day — so `ExternValue::cmp_value` answers `None` and the declaration withholds
// `Comparable`. The negative half of `datetime_ordering_and_display.noe`: without this case, adding
// the trait to `Duration` for symmetry would type-check `a < b` and then fail at run time in both
// backends, which is exactly the failure a declaration is supposed to prevent.
// expect: error E0007 at 15:6
// expect: exit 1
use std.{datetime}

t = datetime.from_unix_ms(0);
a = t.diff(t.add(datetime.hours(1)));
b = t.diff(t.add(datetime.hours(2)));
echo a < b;
